WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.480
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.480
If we wish to populate a virtual world with
photorealistic materials, the last few years

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.030
have offered a number of amazing techniques
to do so.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:18.470
We can obtain such a material from a flash
and no-flash photograph pair of the target

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:24.499
material, and having a neural network create
a digital version of it, or remarkably, even

00:00:24.499 --> 00:00:27.529
just one photograph is enough to perform this.

00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:32.239
This footage that you see here shows these
materials after they have been rendered by

00:00:32.239 --> 00:00:34.260
a light simulation program.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:38.620
If we don't have physical access to these
materials, we can also use a recent learning

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:44.610
algorithm to learn our preferences and recommend
new materials that we would enjoy.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:50.080
However, whenever I publish such a video,
I always get comments asking: "but what about

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:54.540
the more advanced materials?", and my answer
is, you are right!

00:00:54.540 --> 00:01:00.040
Have a look at this piece of work which is
about acquiring printed holographic materials.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:04.449
This means that we have physical access to
this holographic pattern, put a camera close

00:01:04.449 --> 00:01:10.170
by, and measure data in a way that can be
imported into a light simulation program to

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.189
make a digital copy of it.

00:01:12.189 --> 00:01:17.179
This idea is much less far-fetched than it
sounds, because we find such materials in

00:01:17.179 --> 00:01:24.999
many everyday objects, like bank notes, gift
bags, clothing, or of course or security holograms.

00:01:24.999 --> 00:01:27.999
However, it is also quite difficult.

00:01:27.999 --> 00:01:28.999
Look here!

00:01:28.999 --> 00:01:33.670
As you see, these holographic patterns are
quite diverse, and a well-crafted algorithm

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:39.909
would have to be able to capture this rotation
effect, circular diffractive areas, firework

00:01:39.909 --> 00:01:43.570
effects and even this iridescent glitter.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:45.420
That is quite a challenge!

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:49.179
This paper proposes two novel techniques to
approach this problem.

00:01:49.179 --> 00:01:53.869
The first one assumes that there is some sort
of repetition in the visual structure of the

00:01:53.869 --> 00:01:56.060
hologram and takes that into consideration.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:03.630
As a result, it can give us high-quality results
by only taking 1 to 5 photographs of the target

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:04.630
material.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:10.240
The second method is more exhaustive and needs
more specialized hardware, but, in return,

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:16.340
can deal with arbitrary structures, and requires
at least 4 photographs at all times.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:21.590
These are both quite remarkable - just think
about the fact that these materials look different

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:26.840
from every viewing angle, and they also change
over the surface of the object.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:31.250
And for the first technique, we don't need
sophisticated instruments, only a consumer

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:33.790
DSLR camera is required.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:38.470
The reconstructed digital materials can be
used in real time, and what's more, we can

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:44.760
also exert artistic control over the outputs
by modifying the periodicities of the material.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:46.700
How cool is that!

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:51.560
And, if you are about to subscribe to the
series or you are already subscribed, make

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:55.590
sure to click the bell icon, or otherwise,
you may miss future episodes.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:03:00.280
That would be a bummer, because I have a lot
more amazing papers to show you.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:03.230
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

